.TH LAST 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
last \- list recent logins and system boots
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B last
.RB [ \- \fInum\fR]
.RI [ name ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B last
reads the login accounting file
.I /var/log/wtmp
and lists sessions and system boots, most recent first.
.PP
Each login
.RB ( USER_PROCESS
record) is printed on one line containing the user name, the terminal
line, the remote host (or
.B \-
for a local login), and the login time.
The session end is found by searching forward for the next logout
.RB ( DEAD_PROCESS )
record on the same terminal line.
If one is found, the logout time and the session duration in
hours and minutes are appended.
If a boot record comes first, the session is shown as ending at that
boot and is marked
.BR crash .
If neither is found, the session is shown as
.BR "still logged in" .
.PP
Boot records are printed as
.B reboot
on line
.BR "system boot" .
.PP
Times are shown in local time.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \- num
Print at most
.I num
lines.
.B \-0
means no limit.
.TP
.I name
Show only sessions of the user
.IR name .
If
.I name
is
.BR reboot ,
only boot records are shown.
If several names are given, only the last is used.
.PP
Any other argument beginning with
.B \-
is accepted and ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
.I /var/log/wtmp
could not be opened, or memory could not be allocated.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/log/wtmp
Login, logout and boot records, written by
.BR login (1)
and
.BR init .
.SH EXAMPLE
Show the last 10 entries for root:
.PP
.EX
$ last \-10 root
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR id (1),
.BR login (1),
.BR whoami (1)
.SH BUGS
The whole of
.I /var/log/wtmp
is read into memory.
.PP
Sessions whose logout was never written to
.I wtmp
are reported as still logged in until the next boot record.
